  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nTonight \n\nA warm front will move east across northern Minnesota and \nWisconsin. As the warm air advection increases across this area,\nmid-clouds will develop. These clouds will move through north- \ncentral Wisconsin late tonight and help keep temperatures from \ngetting any colder than the mid-30s. As a result, only expecting\npatchy to areas of frost in central and north-central \nWisconsin. With very dry air below 7K feet, the chances for rain\ntonight look to be very low (less than 20 percent). If they do \nhappen to occur, it would be likely north of Wisconsin 29 in\nTaylor County.\n\nLow temperatures tonight will range from the mid-30s to mid-40s.  \n\nSaturday and Saturday night\n\nThe 02.12z models continue to show that a Canadian cold front will \napproach the area from the Northern Plains on Saturday and then\nmove southeast through the area on Saturday night. Like the \npast several days, it looks like the better low-level saturation\nwill be north of Interstate 90. Rain chances in these areas \nwill range from 20-60 percent. Rainfall amounts will range from \na trace to a tenth of an inch.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the mid-60s. Low temperatures will \nrange from the lower 40s to lower 50s.\n\nSunday through Friday\n\nHigh pressure will provide mainly dry weather with near to above-\nnormal temperatures through much of next week. If there is any \nrainfall, it would likely occur from Wednesday into Thursday as a \ncold front moves east through the region. \n\nHigh temperatures will range from the lower 60s to lower 70s. \nLow temperatures will range from the mid-30s to mid-40s early in\nthe week (Sunday and Monday nights) and from the mid-40s to \nmid-50s for the remainder of the week. Temperatures will be cold\nenough in central and north-central Wisconsin that there will \nbe likely some patchy to areas of frost.",
